# Site VPN profiles and legacy OpenVPN credential maintenance

Imports now preserve independent profiles. A controller import identifies its
profile by organization, site, VPN type, controller and server ID. A different
server creates another profile. Reimport updates only the matching profile;
legacy duplicate identities produce an explicit conflict. Imports lock the
parent site to serialize competing first imports through these service paths.
This does not establish a uniqueness constraint across arbitrary SQL writers.

Manual OpenVPN import selects only manual OpenVPN profiles in the requested
organization and site. With several matches, the operator must choose the
profile in the import dialog or supply config_id to /vpn/openvpn/import-config.
It cannot replace a controller-managed profile or convert another VPN type.
Importing stored configuration is not evidence of a successful live tunnel.

New profile creation, site configuration updates and manual imports encrypt
OpenVPN content before storage. Responses omit that write-only content. Older
versions could persist plaintext through the site create/update paths, even
though the standalone connection and manual-import paths encrypted it.

## Existing installations

Upgrade every application writer first and retain a verified encrypted backup
and the ORIGINAL vault SECRET_KEY and ENCRYPTION_SALT. Do not rotate vault keys
to run this maintenance. Use the upgraded application's existing environment,
not a separately configured shell with a new/default key. Inspect the scope in
a qualification copy before applying it to a customer database.

From the configured Compose project, audit without changing profile data:

    docker compose exec -T api python -m scripts.encrypt_vpn_site_configs

After reviewing the audit, explicitly encrypt legacy plaintext:

    docker compose exec -T api python -m scripts.encrypt_vpn_site_configs --apply

The default processes up to 100 batches of 100 nonempty profile values. Each
apply batch takes row locks and commits atomically; a busy row, invalid token or
wrong encryption key stops that batch. Existing ciphertext is verified and left
byte-for-byte unchanged. No profile is connected, deleted or reassigned. Output
contains counts and the last successful UUID cursor, never configuration content.
Exit 0 means the requested scan finished, 1 means failure and 2 means the batch
limit was reached. If incomplete, review the result and resume with --after-id
set to its last_id; --batch-size and --max-batches each accept only 1..1000.
A completed audit can report plaintext without changing it; rerun from the
beginning after maintenance and require plaintext=0 and complete=true.

This changes current database values, not old pages, WAL, replicas, dumps or
snapshots. Treat historical plaintext-bearing copies as sensitive credentials.
Encrypting current rows cannot retract an exposed private key: review access to
old copies and rotate affected VPN identities if those copies were exposed or
outside your control. Do not delete required recovery copies as an automatic
cleanup. Customer backup handling and real tunnel/rotation acceptance remain
operator tasks; this tool is not a secure-erasure or VPN connectivity guarantee.
